::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A rectangular patio is 12 feet long and 5 feet wide. What is its area?
{
=A. 60 square feet#Correct. Area \= length x width \= 12 x 5 \= 60 square feet.
~B. 17 square feet#This adds the dimensions. Area requires multiplication.
~C. 60 cubic feet#The number is correct, but area uses square units.
~D. 34 square feet#This uses perimeter reasoning instead of area.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A box is 6 inches long, 4 inches wide, and 3 inches high. What is its volume?
{
~A. 24 cubic inches#This uses only two dimensions. Volume requires three dimensions.
~B. 13 cubic inches#This adds the dimensions. Volume requires multiplying length, width, and height.
=C. 72 cubic inches#Correct. Volume \= length x width x height \= 6 x 4 x 3.
~D. 72 square inches#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which measurement uses cubic units?
{
~A. area#Area uses square units because it measures flat space.
=B. volume#Correct. Volume uses cubic units because it measures three-dimensional space.
~C. perimeter#Perimeter uses linear units.
~D. length#Length uses one-dimensional units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A cube has side length 5 inches. What is the surface area?
{
~A. 25 square inches#This is the area of one face only. A cube has 6 faces.
~B. 125 square inches#This is the cube volume number with a surface area unit. Surface area needs all faces.
~C. 150 cubic inches#The number is correct for surface area, but the unit should be square inches.
=D. 150 square inches#Correct. One face is 5 x 5 \= 25, and 6 faces give 150 square inches.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which question asks for volume?
{
~A. How much carpet covers a floor?#Carpet covers a flat surface, so this asks for area.
=B. How much soil fills a planter box?#Correct. Filling a box asks for inside space, which is volume.
~C. How much paint covers one wall?#Painting one flat wall asks for area.
~D. How long is the fence?#Length is one-dimensional, not volume.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A rectangle is 8 meters by 7 meters. What is its area?
{
=A. 56 square meters#Correct. Area \= 8 x 7 \= 56 square meters.
~B. 15 square meters#This adds length and width. Area multiplies them.
~C. 30 square meters#This is perimeter for an 8 by 7 rectangle, not area.
~D. 56 cubic meters#Area uses square units, not cubic units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which unit is appropriate for volume?
{
~A. square yards#Square yards measure area. Volume uses cubic units.
~B. yards#Yards measure length only.
~C. pounds#Pounds measure weight or mass, not volume or capacity.
=D. gallons#Correct. Gallons measure volume or capacity.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A storage bin is 10 feet long, 2 feet wide, and 4 feet high. What is the volume?
{
~A. 16 cubic feet#This adds dimensions. Volume requires multiplication.
~B. 20 cubic feet#This uses only length and width. Include height for volume.
=C. 80 cubic feet#Correct. Volume \= 10 x 2 x 4 \= 80 cubic feet.
~D. 80 square feet#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
What does surface area measure?
{
=A. the total outside area of a three-dimensional object#Correct. Surface area adds the areas of outside faces.
~B. the space inside an object#This describes volume.
~C. the distance around a flat shape#This describes perimeter.
~D. the height of an object#Height is one dimension, not surface area.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A square has side length 11 cm. What is its area?
{
~A. 44 square cm#This is the perimeter of the square, not area.
=B. 121 square cm#Correct. Area of a square \= side x side \= 11 x 11 \= 121 square cm.
~C. 22 square cm#This doubles the side length instead of squaring it.
~D. 121 cubic cm#The number is correct, but area uses square units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A rectangular prism has dimensions 3 cm, 5 cm, and 9 cm. What is the volume?
{
~A. 17 cubic cm#This adds the dimensions. Volume multiplies all three.
~B. 45 cubic cm#This uses only 5 x 9. Include the third dimension.
=C. 135 cubic cm#Correct. Volume \= 3 x 5 x 9 \= 135 cubic cm.
~D. 135 square cm#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which problem asks for area?
{
=A. finding the amount of tile needed for a floor#Correct. Tile covers a flat surface, so this asks for area.
~B. finding how much air fills a room#Filling space asks for volume.
~C. finding how much paper wraps a cube#Wrapping all outside faces asks for surface area.
~D. finding how far around a garden fence goes#Distance around is perimeter.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A cube has side length 2 feet. What is its volume?
{
~A. 4 cubic feet#This is one face area, not volume.
~B. 6 cubic feet#This multiplies by the number of faces instead of using side x side x side.
~C. 8 square feet#The number is correct for volume, but the unit should be cubic feet.
=D. 8 cubic feet#Correct. Volume \= 2 x 2 x 2 \= 8 cubic feet.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which formula finds the area of a rectangle?
{
~A. length + width#Adding length and width does not find area.
=B. length x width#Correct. Rectangle area is length times width.
~C. length x width x height#This finds volume for a rectangular prism.
~D. 6 x side x side#This finds surface area of a cube.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A rectangular wall is 13 feet wide and 9 feet high. What is its area?
{
~A. 22 square feet#This adds the dimensions. Area requires multiplication.
~B. 44 square feet#This resembles perimeter reasoning, not area.
=C. 117 square feet#Correct. Area \= 13 x 9 \= 117 square feet.
~D. 117 cubic feet#The number is correct, but wall area uses square units.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which situation most likely requires surface area?
{
~A. filling a fish tank with water#Filling asks for volume.
~B. measuring the length of a board#Length is one-dimensional.
~C. finding the floor space of a classroom#Floor space asks for area.
=D. painting all outside faces of a box#Correct. Painting outside faces asks for surface area.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A prism has a base area of 20 square inches and height 6 inches. What is its volume?
{
=A. 120 cubic inches#Correct. Volume \= base area x height \= 20 x 6 \= 120 cubic inches.
~B. 26 cubic inches#This adds base area and height. Volume requires multiplication.
~C. 120 square inches#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
~D. 60 cubic inches#This uses half of the product without a reason.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
What unit should be used for the surface area of a cube measured in inches?
{
~A. inches#Inches measure length, not surface area.
=B. square inches#Correct. Surface area adds face areas, so it uses square inches.
~C. cubic inches#Cubic inches measure volume, not surface area.
~D. gallons#Gallons measure capacity, not surface area.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A floor is 15 feet by 10 feet. What is the area?
{
~A. 25 square feet#This adds length and width. Area multiplies them.
~B. 50 square feet#This is perimeter reasoning, not area.
~C. 150 cubic feet#The number is correct, but area uses square units.
=D. 150 square feet#Correct. Area \= 15 x 10 \= 150 square feet.
}


::MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A cube has side length 6 cm. What is its surface area?
{
~A. 36 square cm#This is the area of one face only.
~B. 72 square cm#This doubles one face area, but a cube has 6 faces.
=C. 216 square cm#Correct. One face is 6 x 6 \= 36, and 6 faces give 216 square cm.
~D. 216 cubic cm#The number is correct for surface area, but the unit should be square cm.
}

